 "context": "<div data-type=\"note\" data-has-label=\"true\" id=\"eip-696\" class=\"interactive\" data-label=\"\">\n<h3 class=\"os-title\" data-type=\"title\"><span class=\"os-title-label\">PhET Explorations</span></h3>\n<div class=\"os-note-body\">\n<h4 data-type=\"title\" id=\"14\" class=\"os-subtitle\"><span class=\"os-subtitle-label\">PhET Explorations: Wave Interference</span></h4>\n<p id=\"eip-id2942273\" class=\" \">Make waves with a dripping faucet, audio speaker, or laser! Add a second source or a pair of slits to create an interference pattern.</p>\n <div class=\"os-figure\">\n<figure id=\"eip-id2746277\"><div data-type=\"media\" id=\"fs-id1167067067106\" data-alt=\"This simulation allows you to make waves with a dripping faucet, audio speaker, or laser. You can also add a second source to create an interference pattern, put up a barrier to explore single-slit diffraction and double-slit interference, and experiment with diffraction through elliptical, rectangular, or irregular apertures.\">\n<div data-type=\"alternatives\" class=\"os-has-iframe os-has-link\">\n<a class=\"os-is-link\" target=\"_window\" href=\"https://openstax.org/l/28interference\">Click to view content</a><iframe width=\"660\" height=\"371.4\" src=\"https://openstax.org/l/28interference\" class=\"os-is-iframe\"><!-- no-selfclose --></iframe>\n</div>\n</div></figure><div class=\"os-caption-container\">\n<span class=\"os-title-label\">Figure </span><span class=\"os-number\">16.46</span><span class=\"os-divider\"> </span>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>",
 "stimulus_html": "",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 239024,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "PhET Explorations: Wave Interference.\n\nMake waves with a dripping faucet, audio speaker, or laser! Add a second source or a pair of slits to create an interference pattern.\n\n<iframe src=\"https://openstax.org/l/28interference\"></iframe>",
   "stem_html": "In the water tab, compare the waves generated by one drip versus two drips. What happens to the amplitude of the waves when there are two drips? Is this constructive or destructive interference? Why would this be the case?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 745780,
     "content_html": "The amplitude of the water waves remains same because of the destructive interference as the drips of water hit the surface at the same time."
    },
    {
     "id": 745779,
     "content_html": "The amplitude of the water waves is canceled because of the destructive interference as the drips of water hit the surface at the same time."
    },
    {
     "id": 745778,
     "content_html": "The amplitude of water waves remains same because of the constructive interference as the drips of water hit the surface at the same time."
    },
    {
     "id": 745781,
     "content_html": "The amplitude of water waves doubles because of the constructive interference as the drips of water hit the surface at the same time."
    }
   ],
